TARGET BRANCH:
switch (((unsigned char)ptr[0] << 8) | (unsigned char)ptr[1]) == 0x3C00 -> take case block in xmltok.c:initScan()

JUSTIFICATION:
This branch detects UTF-16 Little Endian input without a BOM (first two bytes 0x3C 0x00, i.e., '<' followed by NUL). It has zero historical coverage (lines 1200-1205: 0/6, 0%). It steers the parser onto the UTF-16LE path (little2_ vtable and converters), exercising distinct logic from the current ASCII/UTF-8 path. It is reachable with the existing harness by supplying stdin that begins with 0x3C 0x00 and continues as UTF-16LE pairs, thereby significantly improving coverage in encoding detection and conversion routines.

EXPECTED COVERED LINES:
xmltok.c:1201-1202

PATH CONSTRAINT:
Input Source and Delivery:
- The program reads XML data from standard input (stdin). Provide data via stdin only; no command-line arguments influence encoding detection.

Data Encoding and Byte-Level Structure:
- Supply the entire stdin stream encoded as UTF-16 Little Endian (UTF-16LE) without a Byte Order Mark (BOM). This means every Unicode code unit is two bytes: low-order byte first, then high-order byte.
- The very first two bytes of stdin must be 0x3C 0x00 (UTF-16LE encoding of the character '<'). This triggers the 0x3C00 case in the encoding detection switch.

Minimum Length Requirement:
- Ensure stdin contains at least 2 bytes (the initial pair) followed by additional UTF-16LE pairs forming a syntactically valid start of an XML document (e.g., "<" plus a name and closing ">"), so that the scanner continues after detection. Avoid a single 2-byte input because downstream logic expects more content.

XML Well-Formedness (High-Level, Encoding-Agnostic):
- After the initial '<' (0x3C 0x00), provide characters that form a valid XML prolog or element start. Characters must be representable in UTF-16LE and adhere to XML name rules to prevent immediate tokenizer errors. Do not include a UTF-8 BOM sequence (EF BB BF) or any NUL-separated bytes that would suggest UTF-16BE.

Environmental Conditions:
- No special environment variables or files are needed. The executing binary must read stdin without truncation. Ensure no upstream filter converts or re-encodes the stream. Maintain byte alignment: total byte count must be even, as UTF-16LE consists of 2-byte units.

Forbidden/Edge Cases to Avoid:
- Do not prepend a BOM (FF FE or FE FF), which would route execution to other switch cases.
- Do not start with 0x00 0x3C (UTF-16BE form of '<'), which targets a different branch.
- Do not start with 0xEF 0xBB (beginning of a UTF-8 BOM), which routes to the EFBB case.
- Avoid mixing encodings or inserting isolated high-surrogate values (0xD800–0xDBFF) without a proper low-surrogate pair; keep code units in valid ranges when using non-ASCII characters.

Chronological Summary of Required Constraints:
1) Byte 0 (first byte of stdin) = 0x3C.
2) Byte 1 (second byte of stdin) = 0x00.
3) All subsequent bytes form valid UTF-16LE code unit pairs (even total length), representing a well-formed beginning of an XML document.
4) No BOM bytes at start; specifically, the first two bytes must not be 0xFF 0xFE or 0xFE 0xFF.
5) The input must not begin with the UTF-8 BOM prefix 0xEF 0xBB.
6) Avoid invalid surrogate usage; if surrogate pairs are used, they must be correctly paired and produce valid Unicode scalar values.

